This was a very, very, bones-friendly game; while I only saw five bones 
levels, two early ones of wizards provided a few nice tools to ensure a 
better early-game survival rate (MR and "oESP most notably), as well as 
an athame (well, okay, Magicbane, but the plain athame the second one got 
converted to would have been fine) for noteleaving purposes.

With both MR and a fair bit of combat survivability, I did Mines before 
Sobokan--I'm thinking this might be a good general policy for me in the 
future, as things went much better than usual. Bones pile 3 was on the up 
stairs of the first level of the Mines; while there was nothing hugely 
important in it, I was not going to say no to a nice oil lamp.

Other than that, the Mines were fairly uneventful; the priest in Minetown 
was coaligned, Izchak had no interesting lamps (but I did purchase a very 
lovely set of candles from him). Mine's End was the Mimic of the Mines, 
which worked out quite well with the help of ESP.

The rest of the Dungeon proper was fairly boring; I found the Quest 
portal on the way down, but was significantly short on experience. 
Bigroom happened, but wasn't tricky to deal with. I didn't make a point 
of looking for Fort Ludios; there's at least one vault I didn't get to, 
so it could have been in the game.

A sufficient kit of stuff to deal with the Medusa level had shown up by 
the time I got down there; the titan was generated peaceful, so the only 
scary moment I had was noticing a lich on one of the islands. Can't have 
the boots of water walking destroyed, can we? Blessed genoed L's.

Finished diving down to the Castle (for the first time ever--in fact, you 
may as well mentally append "for the first time ever" to the rest of the 
accomplishments this game ;), but didn't feel at all ready to start in on 
it; in the maze level between Medusa and the Castle, though, I'd hit 14, 
so it was off to the Quest.

Bones level four was in the middle here, as was my first magic marker of 
the game (although not in the pile itself). Surtur was kind of a 
pushover, and indeed I did spend more time worrying about drawbridges 
than the giant himself. Unfortunately, trying to tote the Orb of Fate 
meant leaving some of the stuff from the most recent bones file back 
where it was.

At this point, I consolidated stuff, and decided I wasn't really ready to 
face the Castle for the first time--so I played some random games of 
Slash'em to take my mind off of things. One of them lasted longer than I 
expected, and, well.. I've posted some of the results of that. I got her 
down to Rodney, though, and decided I wasn't ready for that, either.. so 
back I came!

The Castle was actually a lot easier than I expected. Again, drawbridge 
paranoia struck, not that I knew the passtune. Or had an instrument. Ah 
well, strike one drawbridge. Once inside and done with the horde, I 
quickly go for the WoW: I may have been wishless so far, but I'm not 
going for conducts. My wishes are simple, and fairly typical: scrolls of 
charging, SDSM (so I can free up my neck slot for a AoLS), AoLS (.. 
what?), gauntlets of power (sure, I had a pair.. but there's a fair 
difference between -2 rusty and +2 rustproof), a blessed magic marker (at 
the time, I was short on holy water), helm of telepathy.. oh yeah, a 
source of levitation would probably be a good idea in the near future, 
wouldn't it? Ring of levitation.

There's nothing too interesting in the Castle storerooms (well, another 
pair of gauntlets of power; kinda wish I'd looked before wishing), so 
it's down to the Valley we drop. I start next to a fair-sized horde, but 
I'm not too worried. I should've been, though: there were enough monsters 
there that I'd been dropped 7 squares away from the upstairs. That's 
right, this is the fifth and final bones file I'd encounter; as it would 
turn out, there were two poor souls who both got mobbed nearer the 
stairs. The first pile I inspected was a fairly normal (for this deep) 
set of barbarian remains; I didn't really need a neutral Heart or the 
Vorpal Blade, so most of his stuff (minus a magic lamp he'd been using 
for a lightsource) stayed there. Well, except for four magic markers.

The other pile, though... this valkyrie (I hope) had managed to die with 
three magic lamps and a wand of wishing (0:2) in her bag of holding. .. 
but where was the crystal ball/former Orb? The barbarian might have had 
it (he had a crystal ball in his bag).. but then why were all the wands 
and lamps still in her set of stuff? I don't know.

In any event, not inclined to let this huge pile of stuff go to waste, I 
started in with a couple more wishes (new cloak of MR, t-shirt) and a lot 
of scroll-writing. I'd never even /dreamed/ -42 AC before. Finding out 
the big pile of water in the valk's bag had stayed holy water (after 
using wishes from the wand, of course. grumble.) helped a lot there as 
well.

So, feeling far too invincible, down we go! None of the lairs were 
particularly interesting. Vlad's tower was easy to find, and didn't 
contain much of a threat, either. One vaguely interesting thing, though, 
the long sword in one of the niches was actually generated as Demonbane. 
Odd place to keep it.

So, time for the final dive to the bottom, digging nice little paths as 
we go. Surprisingly, Mojo seems not to like shattering wands of digging, 
even if it's blasted every wand of death a monster's had to shreds. The 
VS doesn't hide very well, so it's back up to meet Rodney. (Dun dun DUN! 
.. sorry.) The Gazetteer is close at hand at this point, so it's not too 
difficult to find my way up the tower. The zoo in the middle gives me a 
bit of worry, though, and I ended up having to genocide disenchanters so 
as not to waste blank scrolls.

Rodney himself takes 3 WoD zaps. Grumble.

Right, back down to the VS. Before going in, I use my one and only potion 
of unholy water to curse my potions of gain level. Very handy, that is. 
The Sanctum itself.. again, it felt like a pushover. This feeling was 
made stronger when I went to face off against the High Priest of Moloch. 
I tried the wand of death first, of course. I see the resistance glyph 
sequence, presumably nothing's going to happen. 

Moloch's voice booms: "How darest thou harm my servant!" A bolt of 
lightning strikes you! But it reflects from your armor! A bolt of 
lightning strikes the high priest of Moloch! You killed the high priest 
of Moloch!

Er, what?

Well, okay, I'm not going to complain too much about that. Snag the AoY, 
name it, and RUN. Well, quaff.

And, the RNG finally decides it's time to lower the boom. It takes 56 
staircases (and one more c!oGL, in a particularly awkward placement on a 
no-teleport level) to go 20 levels up. Amazingly, Rodney comes back 
exactly once during all of this, and does nothing else of note. He takes 
another four zaps, though.

.. and that's really it. The rest of the Dungeons were cake, and so were 
the Elemental planes.

Earth: Rodney takes another three zaps (but one gets the Elvenking on the 
bounce, so I guess it wasn't wasted). I have enough wands of digging to 
excavate the entire plane if need be, but it doesn't really seem 
necessary.

Air: I consider, and even attempt, meleeing my way through the air 
elementals. While they were having a hard time killing me, 7 points of 
damage per action is still more than I want to take. Plus, I'm getting 
kind of mobbed. Sigh, fine, wands of death and flee forward it is.

Fire: *snicker* The fire traps are clearly marked, I'm already levitating 
over the lava, and I can apparently outrun everything (and will, since 
Air got me in the right mindset).

Water: The Orb of Fate finally needed a recharge here; luckily, I still 
had a blessed scroll of charging left. After that, I pondered the 
situation a bit. I wasn't confident that levitation was enough to avoid 
being pulled underwater (rightfully so?), so pulled out scroll, potion of 
water (water, water everywhere, but not a handy place to #dip), potion of 
holy water and marker and finally got around to genoing ;'s. 

Rodney also finally /did/ something, cursing some of my items. Well, 2 of 
them. Well, actually, one was only unblessed. And the actually-cursed 
item was a ring of free action I had no intention of taking off ever 
anyway. *sigh*

Astral: finally, something interesting. Telepathy showed Death left, 
Pestilence middle, Famine right. Famine seems easiest, so I start plowing 
through. I don't really fear the player-monsters (Vorpy's back at the 
VotD, remember?), although I only actually saw one of them.

Over to the door, Famine goes down. Next door, there's a nice big mass of 
A's and @'s waiting for me. Almost as though Rodney tipped them off.  
While I'm trying to break through the mass, Famine comes back for round 
two. Crud, I'm out of WoD charges. Well, I can wish up a couple more 
wands, let's do so. He gets up again before enough priests go down to let 
me slip through.. chaotic. Great. Famine takes another shot, then one of 
the priests is kind enough to summon insects to cover my escape.

Center! One of the priests decides to play with cursed create monster 
rather than summon insects; he manages to get a master mind flayer, which 
I take as a personal affront. Unfortunately, it takes a number of wand 
zaps to finally get him from afar. I wade out into the middle after 
zapping Pestilence (who also dodges a truely annoying number of shots) 
and read taming, but only three of them convert. Before I can get through 
the crowd, Pestilence is back, and dodges my remaining wand zaps. I 
probably don't have enough time to pull out scrolls to write charging 
with.. aw, what the heck. VALK SMASH!.. and he goes down in two rounds. 
I'm not sure if he'd actually been damaged by prior cover fire from angry 
priests with wands of magic missile or not, but either way maybe I should 
consider intentionally leaving wands for them to use in the future.

Slipping inside to the high altar here is easier, and, as you may have 
guessed from the map, it was indeed the lawful altar.

Can you still use wishes at the bar? I've heard rumors that it's really 
BYO!...

